# Line-ending declaration for shell scripts.
#
# This fixes nothing. Every tracked *.sh is ALREADY stored LF and checks out
# LF, verified byte-exactly (`git cat-file blob HEAD:<path> | tr -dc '\r' |
# wc -c` reports 0 for all 81, working copies too). Re-adding all of them
# under this rule produced no blob change.
#
# It is here to PIN that, because the correctness currently depends on how
# each contributor's git happens to be configured. core.autocrlf=true on
# Windows hands most text files to the working copy as CRLF; these are the
# files where that would be wrong, and nothing in the repository says so.
# A CRLF *.sh does not run on macOS or Linux -- the shebang ends in a
# carriage return, so the kernel looks for an interpreter named "bash\r".
# That reaches users, not just developers: templates/**/build.sh is the
# documented way to compile a single template, and macOS is the reference
# platform.
#
# Declared once here, the behaviour no longer depends on a local setting.

*.sh    text eol=lf

# Two omissions, both deliberate and both measured rather than assumed.
#
#  * *.bat is NOT declared. Those 71 blobs are stored WITH CRLF, which is
#    correct for batch files. Adding `*.bat text eol=crlf` normalises them to
#    LF in the index -- the opposite of the intent -- so the rule that looks
#    right is the one that does damage. Left alone on purpose.
#
#  * `* text=auto` is NOT declared. It would make git reclassify the PNGs,
#    the .npz archives, the .mayaSwatches blobs and 58 Maya ASCII scenes,
#    churning the index for no benefit. Git's own binary detection already
#    handles every one of them correctly.
